---
import ProjectLayout from '../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
import SectionHead from '../../components/SectionHead.astro';
import Panel from '../../components/Panel.astro';
import ev from '../../data/homelab-evidence.json';

// Every number here comes from src/data/homelab-evidence.json (read-only
// checks on the live hypervisor, dated) or from a dated incident named next to
// it. Nothing is illustrative except the watchdog demo, which runs the real
// rules on sample jobs and says so.
const g = ev.guests;
const sh = ev.selfheal;
const wd = ev.watchdog;
const [r1, r2] = ev.restores;
const mmss = (s: number) => (s < 60 ? `${s} s` : `${Math.floor(s / 60)} min ${s % 60} s`);
---

<ProjectLayout
  title="Homelab"
  path="/projects/homelab/"
  description="A Proxmox VE node run like production: timed restores, a watchdog that notices when anything goes quiet, health probes that repair what they safely can, and fail-closed VPN egress."
  lede="One Proxmox node, held to production standards. Backups get restored on a stopwatch, a watchdog notices when anything goes quiet, probes repair what they safely can, and the workloads that must never leak fail closed. The useful part is what broke along the way, and what I changed."
  tags={['Proxmox', 'ZFS', 'nftables', 'Network namespaces', 'Caddy', 'Python', 'Linux ops']}
  kicker="Infrastructure"
  headline="A homelab run like production"
  accent="like production"
  slug="homelab"
  stats={[
    { n: String(g.containers), l: `containers on ${g.running} guests` },
    { n: String(wd.checks), l: 'jobs and feeds watched for silence' },
    { n: String(sh.probes), l: `health probes every ${sh.every_min} minutes` },
    { n: mmss(r1.seconds), l: 'timed restore of the SIEM VM' },
  ]}
  demonstrates="Virtualization, storage design, backup and recovery discipline, monitoring that checks itself, safe automation, and root-cause analysis with real counters. Same control mindset as the day job, applied at home."
>
  <div slot="hero-aside" data-canvas>
    <div class="hero-canvas">
      <div class="hero-canvas-head">
        <span class="label">homelab · live topology</span>
        <span class="live"><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>sanitized</span>
      </div>
      <canvas
        data-topology
        role="img"
        aria-label="Diagram of a self-hosted homelab: a hypervisor at the centre linked to storage, a GPU media VM, containers, a VPN namespace, a TLS proxy, monitoring and nightly backups."
      ></canvas>
    </div>
  </div>

  <SectionHead label="The map" title="Architecture" />
  <p>
    This is what runs on it: {g.running} guests ({g.vms} VMs, {g.lxcs} LXC
    containers) carrying {g.containers} Docker containers. Atlas, my private
    voice for alerts, and <a href="/projects/jarvis/">Jarvis</a>, a public bot,
    live here, one trusted and one deliberately not, and so does my
    <a href="/projects/mealie/">Mealie fork</a>. One GPU does four jobs. The
    security controls in front of all of it have
    <a href="/projects/security/">their own page</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>In one line:</strong> everything reaches the internet through one
    hardened front door, and the apps that must stay private are blocked
    from every other way out. Run the scenarios below to watch it, or click
    any box for what it runs and why.
  </p>

  <Panel label="homelab · traffic map" status="trace it">
    <div class="diagram" data-map>
      <p class="scn-lead" data-js-only>Pick a scenario and watch the packet move.</p>
      <div class="scn-grid" data-js-only role="group" aria-label="Scenarios">
        <button class="scn is-hint" type="button" data-trace="normal" aria-pressed="false">
          <span class="scn-play" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="scn-t">Normal traffic</span>
          <span class="scn-d">An ordinary service reaches the internet</span>
        </button>
        <button class="scn" type="button" data-trace="isolated" aria-pressed="false">
          <span class="scn-play" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="scn-t">Locked to the VPN</span>
          <span class="scn-d">Isolated apps can only leave through the tunnel</span>
        </button>
        <button class="scn scn-red" type="button" data-trace="bypass" aria-pressed="false">
          <span class="scn-play" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="scn-t">Try to escape</span>
          <span class="scn-d">An isolated app tries the home connection</span>
        </button>
        <button class="scn scn-red" type="button" data-trace="trust" aria-pressed="false">
          <span class="scn-play" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="scn-t">Hack the public bot</span>
          <span class="scn-d">Can it reach the house from there?</span>
        </button>
      </div>
      <div class="scn-switch-row" data-js-only>
        <button class="scn-switch" type="button" data-toggle="tunnel" aria-pressed="false">
          <span class="scn-knob" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span>VPN tunnel: <strong data-tunnel-state>up</strong></span>
        </button>
        <span class="scn-hint">Pull the plug, then run "Locked to the VPN" again.</span>
      </div>

      <svg viewBox="0 0 980 630" role="group" aria-label="Homelab architecture map" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <!-- Ghost routes: paths something wants to take and cannot. Never complete. -->
        <path
          id="ghost-bypass"
          class="route-ghost"
          d="M 366 422 H 410 V 200 C 410 168 700 144 805 144"
        ></path>
        <path
          id="ghost-trust"
          class="route-ghost"
          d="M 382 486 H 415 V 270 H 690 V 286"
        ></path>

        <!-- Real topology, always visible -->
        <path
          id="route-normal"
          class="route route-base"
          d="M 535 320 V 290 H 490 V 180 C 490 158 700 144 805 144 V 56 Q 805 36 784 36 H 585"
        ></path>
        <path
          id="route-isolated"
          class="route route-base"
          d="M 233 402 V 170 Q 233 144 207 144 H 175 V 56 Q 175 36 196 36 H 395"
        ></path>

        <!-- Failure-state routes -->
        <path id="route-bypass" class="route is-blocked" d="M 366 422 H 382"></path>
        <path id="route-tunneldown" class="route is-blocked" d="M 233 402 V 388"></path>
        <path id="route-trust" class="route is-blocked" d="M 382 486 H 398"></path>

        <!-- Off-host -->
        <g class="node" data-node="internet" role="button" tabindex="0">
          <rect class="node-box" x="395" y="16" width="190" height="40" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="node-title" x="490" y="42" text-anchor="middle">Internet</text>
        </g>

        <g class="node" data-node="vpn-endpoint" role="button" tabindex="0">
          <rect class="node-box" x="70" y="100" width="210" height="44" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="node-title" x="175" y="120" text-anchor="middle">VPN provider endpoint</text>
          <text class="node-sub" x="175" y="136" text-anchor="middle">sole egress path</text>
        </g>

        <g class="node" data-node="gateway" role="button" tabindex="0">
          <rect class="node-box" x="700" y="100" width="210" height="44" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="node-title" x="805" y="120" text-anchor="middle">LAN gateway</text>
          <text class="node-sub" x="805" y="136" text-anchor="middle">namespace firewall drops it</text>
        </g>

        <!-- Host -->
        <g class="node" data-node="hypervisor" role="button" tabindex="0">
          <rect class="host-box" x="40" y="180" width="900" height="430" rx="10"></rect>
          <text class="host-label" x="56" y="202">Hypervisor · Proxmox VE</text>
        </g>

        <g class="node" data-node="proxy" role="button" tabindex="0">
          <rect class="node-box" x="390" y="212" width="200" height="46" rx="7"></rect>
          <text class="node-title" x="490" y="232" text-anchor="middle">Proxy VM</text>
          <text class="node-sub" x="490" y="248" text-anchor="middle">Caddy · TLS terminates here</text>
        </g>

        <!-- Container host: isolated namespace + the apps -->
        <g class="node" data-node="container-host" role="button" tabindex="0">
          <rect class="node-box" x="68" y="286" width="330" height="248" rx="7"></rect>
          <text class="node-title" x="84" y="308" text-anchor="start">Container host (LXC)</text>
        </g>

        <rect class="vpn-box" x="84" y="320" width="298" height="132" rx="6"></rect>
        <text class="vpn-label" x="233" y="338" text-anchor="middle">egress-controlled namespace</text>

        <g class="node" data-node="tunnel" role="button" tabindex="0">
          <rect class="node-box" x="100" y="350" width="266" height="36" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="node-title" x="233" y="373" text-anchor="middle">VPN tunnel</text>
        </g>

        <g class="node" data-node="workloads" role="button" tabindex="0">
          <rect class="node-box" x="100" y="402" width="266" height="40" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="node-title" x="233" y="419" text-anchor="middle">Isolated workloads</text>
          <text class="node-sub" x="233" y="435" text-anchor="middle">default-drop firewall</text>
        </g>

        <g class="node" data-node="mealie" role="button" tabindex="0">
          <rect class="node-box" x="84" y="462" width="142" height="48" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="node-title" x="155" y="482" text-anchor="middle">Mealie</text>
          <text class="node-sub" x="155" y="498" text-anchor="middle">my fork, my builds</text>
        </g>

        <g class="node" data-node="jarvis" role="button" tabindex="0">
          <rect class="node-box" x="240" y="462" width="142" height="48" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="node-title" x="311" y="482" text-anchor="middle">Jarvis</text>
          <text class="node-sub" x="311" y="498" text-anchor="middle">public bot · no HA keys</text>
        </g>

        <!-- Media VM: GPU does double duty -->
        <g class="node" data-node="media" role="button" tabindex="0">
          <rect class="node-box" x="420" y="286" width="230" height="200" rx="7"></rect>
          <text class="node-title" x="436" y="308" text-anchor="start">Media VM</text>
          <text class="node-sub" x="436" y="324" text-anchor="start">RTX 3070 passed through</text>
        </g>

        <g class="node" data-node="jellyfin" role="button" tabindex="0">
          <rect class="node-box" x="436" y="338" width="198" height="44" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="node-title" x="535" y="357" text-anchor="middle">Playback</text>
          <text class="node-sub" x="535" y="373" text-anchor="middle">NVENC transcoding</text>
        </g>

        <g class="node" data-node="voice" role="button" tabindex="0">
          <rect class="node-box" x="436" y="398" width="198" height="50" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="node-title" x="535" y="418" text-anchor="middle">AI jobs</text>
          <text class="node-sub" x="535" y="434" text-anchor="middle">speech · LLM · upscale</text>
        </g>

        <text class="node-sub" x="535" y="470" text-anchor="middle">one GPU, four jobs</text>

        <!-- Right column -->
        <g class="node" data-node="friday" role="button" tabindex="0">
          <rect class="node-box" x="672" y="286" width="248" height="52" rx="7"></rect>
          <text class="node-title" x="796" y="308" text-anchor="middle">Atlas</text>
          <text class="node-sub" x="796" y="325" text-anchor="middle">Home Assistant · private voice</text>
        </g>

        <g class="node" data-node="requests" role="button" tabindex="0">
          <rect class="node-box" x="672" y="356" width="248" height="48" rx="7"></rect>
          <text class="node-title" x="796" y="376" text-anchor="middle">Requests</text>
          <text class="node-sub" x="796" y="392" text-anchor="middle">user-facing intake</text>
        </g>

        <g class="node" data-node="dashboard" role="button" tabindex="0">
          <rect class="node-box" x="672" y="422" width="248" height="48" rx="7"></rect>
          <text class="node-title" x="796" y="442" text-anchor="middle">Dashboard</text>
          <text class="node-sub" x="796" y="458" text-anchor="middle">verdict first · six pages</text>
        </g>

        <g class="node" data-node="monitoring" role="button" tabindex="0">
          <rect class="node-box" x="672" y="488" width="248" height="48" rx="7"></rect>
          <text class="node-title" x="796" y="508" text-anchor="middle">Monitoring</text>
          <text class="node-sub" x="796" y="524" text-anchor="middle">Netdata · watchdog · self-heal</text>
        </g>

        <g class="node" data-node="storage" role="button" tabindex="0">
          <rect class="storage-box" x="68" y="548" width="852" height="48" rx="7"></rect>
          <text class="storage-title" x="84" y="569" text-anchor="start">ZFS storage</text>
          <text class="storage-sub" x="84" y="585" text-anchor="start">three pools · guests on a mirror · timed restores</text>
        </g>

        <!-- Animated packet + block marker (last, so they paint on top) -->
        <circle id="packet" r="5" cx="0" cy="0"></circle>
        <g id="block-mark">
          <circle class="bm-burst" cx="0" cy="0" r="9"></circle>
          <circle cx="0" cy="0" r="9"></circle>
          <line x1="-4" y1="-4" x2="4" y2="4"></line>
          <line x1="4" y1="-4" x2="-4" y2="4"></line>
          <text class="bm-label" x="14" y="4">dropped</text>
        </g>
      </svg>

      <p class="node-hint">Click a box, or focus it and press Enter.</p>

      <div class="node-panel" data-panel aria-live="polite">
        <p>Select a component, or trace a path, to see how egress is controlled.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="node-descriptions">
        <article data-desc="internet">
          <h3>Internet</h3>
          <p>
            Two egress paths reach it, and they are not interchangeable. Ordinary
            services leave through the LAN gateway. Isolated workloads can only
            reach it through the VPN provider, because the firewall inside their
            namespace drops everything else.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article data-desc="vpn-endpoint">
          <h3>VPN provider endpoint</h3>
          <p>
            The single internet egress for the isolated namespace. Anything else
            headed for the internet is dropped before it leaves the namespace, so
            this is not the preferred path out. It is the only one allowed.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article data-desc="gateway">
          <h3>LAN gateway</h3>
          <p>
            The normal route to the internet for ordinary services. The isolated
            workloads cannot use it to get out: their namespace's firewall drops
            by default and accepts only the tunnel, the VPN handshake, and the
            local subnet the media stack talks to.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article data-desc="hypervisor">
          <h3>Hypervisor · Proxmox VE</h3>
          <p>
            One host, carved into VMs and LXC containers. Every guest carries its
            own default-deny firewall rather than relying on the cluster firewall,
            so the host is not the only thing between a service and the network,
            and guests stay portable.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article data-desc="container-host">
          <h3>Container host (LXC)</h3>
          <p>
            Runs most of the containerized apps. The ones that must only ever use
            the VPN share one network namespace with the VPN container instead of
            having a network of their own.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article data-desc="tunnel">
          <h3>VPN tunnel</h3>
          <p>
            Everything the isolated workloads send to the internet leaves through
            here. If it drops, they do not fall back to the home connection, they
            lose the internet until it comes back, which is the correct failure
            mode. I killed it six times to check: 45 probes during the outages,
            none answered, and it recovered on its own in 23 to 61 seconds.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article data-desc="workloads">
          <h3>Isolated workloads</h3>
          <p>
            Containers that need internet access but must never use the home
            connection. They share the VPN container's network namespace, where
            the firewall's default is drop. A leak would need a rule that is not
            there, and I check that by killing the tunnel, not by reading the
            config.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article data-desc="proxy">
          <h3>Proxy VM · Caddy</h3>
          <p>
            All TLS terminates here and every service sits behind it. Nothing is
            exposed directly, so there is one front door to harden instead of a
            dozen.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article data-desc="requests">
          <h3>Requests LXC</h3>
          <p>
            The user-facing intake, kept separate from the workloads that act on
            what it receives. A blast-radius decision: the part strangers touch
            holds the API keys it needs to pass a request on, but has no media
            library and none of the apps that act on requests.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article data-desc="media">
          <h3>Media VM · GPU passthrough</h3>
          <p>
            The RTX 3070 is passed through to this VM alone and does four jobs:
            NVENC transcoding for playback, speech for the voice stack, a local
            language model for translation, and image upscaling. Transcoding wins:
            the upscaler pauses whenever a stream holds an NVENC session, and waits
            for the card to cool from {ev.upscaler.hot_c} °C to {ev.upscaler.cool_c} °C before it continues.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article data-desc="jellyfin">
          <h3>Playback · NVENC</h3>
          <p>
            Hardware transcoding on the 3070. This is the job the GPU was bought
            for. Everything else on the card is batch work that gets out of its
            way.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article data-desc="voice">
          <h3>AI jobs · speech, translation, upscaling</h3>
          <p>
            Whisper for speech-to-text and Kokoro for the voice, a local model for
            translation, and an upscaler. All of it runs on the same card, with no
            cloud API in the loop. Measuring the speech pipeline is what showed that
            text-to-speech, the stage that sounds slow, is the fastest part of a
            response.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article data-desc="friday">
          <h3>Atlas · the private voice</h3>
          <p>
            Home Assistant runs the house, in its own container. Atlas is the voice
            on top: a watcher on the hypervisor reads health, security and self-heal
            state every {ev.atlas.watch_every_s} seconds, and Home Assistant decides what gets said and
            where, on my PC when I'm at it, otherwise my phone. Only I hear it,
            which is why it is allowed to know all of this.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article data-desc="jarvis">
          <h3>Jarvis · the public bot</h3>
          <p>
            The Discord bot my friends talk to. It runs on the container host with
            {ev.jarvis.tools} tools (music, polls, timers, web search, media requests), and
            none of them touch the house: there is no Home Assistant credential in
            its environment and no Home Assistant code in its image.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article data-desc="mealie">
          <h3>Mealie · my fork</h3>
          <p>
            A fork of the recipe manager that I build and deploy here, with an
            acceptance check on every change. Running what I build is the point:
            every regression I ship lands on the machine I use at dinner.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article data-desc="dashboard">
          <h3>Dashboard LXC</h3>
          <p>
            Six pages that lead with a one-line verdict: health, security, media,
            books, a monthly tool radar, news. Acknowledgements happen on the page
            with an undo, and actions are signed links. My phone reads a summary of
            it over a private tunnel.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article data-desc="monitoring">
          <h3>Monitoring · Netdata, watchdog, self-heal</h3>
          <p>
            Netdata keeps per-second metrics. On top of it, a watchdog checks that
            {wd.checks} jobs and feeds are still updating, and {sh.probes} health probes repair what they
            safely can. Both are explained below. An AI assistant can read all of
            it through read-only tokens; any change it proposes waits for my go.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article data-desc="storage">
          <h3>ZFS storage · three pools</h3>
          <p>
            Guest disks live on a two-disk mirror, the media library on a second
            mirror. Nightly backups go to a separate disk and a weekly copy goes to
            the media mirror, so one dead disk never takes a guest and its only
            backup together.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Panel>

  <SectionHead label="Watchdog" title="Nothing gets registered by hand" />
  <p>
    <strong>In one line:</strong> if anything stops updating, I hear about it
    before I would ever notice it myself. Monitoring usually answers "is it up?". The failure I care about more is
    quieter: a scan that stopped running, a backup that stopped landing, a
    dashboard that shows last Tuesday as if it were today. Every 10 minutes a
    watchdog finds every scheduled job on all eight hosts and every data feed
    the dashboard reads, by itself, and checks each one is still updating.
    Right now that is {wd.checks} checks, {wd.fresh} of them fresh.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The limit comes from the schedule.</strong> A timer or cron job
      goes stale at twice its interval plus a margin, so a job that runs every
      6 hours is late after 12 h 10 min, not at a number I picked. Feeds with a
      known producer get a limit that matches it.</li>
    <li><strong>New jobs are covered the moment they exist.</strong> A data feed
      the watchdog cannot match to a job is itself flagged: "no refresher
      registered". That is how it finds gaps instead of waiting for me to
      remember them.</li>
    <li><strong>It repairs only what is safe to repeat.</strong> A stale feed
      with an idempotent generator is re-run once, at most every
      {wd.heal_once_per_h} hours, before anyone is alerted. Everything else goes
      straight to an alert. It never restarts or rebuilds anything.</li>
  </ul>

  <Panel label="watchdog · sample run" status="fast-forward it">
    <div class="wd" data-wd>
      <div class="map-controls" data-js-only>
        <button class="map-btn" type="button" data-wd-step="60">+1 hour</button>
        <button class="map-btn" type="button" data-wd-step="720">+12 hours</button>
        <button class="map-btn" type="button" data-wd-stop="hostbak">stop the config backup</button>
        <button class="map-btn" type="button" data-wd-stop="health">stop the health feed</button>
        <button class="map-btn" type="button" data-wd-add="cron">add a cron job</button>
        <button class="map-btn" type="button" data-wd-add="feed">add an unknown feed</button>
        <button class="map-btn" type="button" data-wd-reset>reset</button>
      </div>
      <p class="wd-clock"><span class="label">clock</span> <span data-wd-clock>+0 h 00 min</span> <span class="wd-sum" data-wd-sum></span></p>
      <div class="map-table-wrap">
      <table class="ev-table wd-table">
        <caption>Sample jobs with the watchdog's real limit rules. Names are generic; the rules are the ones in production.</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Job or feed</th><th scope="col">Runs</th><th scope="col">Stale after</th><th scope="col">Age</th><th scope="col">State</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody data-wd-rows>
          <tr><th scope="row">Dashboard health feed</th><td>every 10 min</td><td>30 min</td><td>0 min</td><td>fresh</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Self-heal status</th><td>every 10 min</td><td>25 min</td><td>0 min</td><td>fresh</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Container vulnerability scan</th><td>daily</td><td>26 h</td><td>0 min</td><td>fresh</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Hypervisor config backup</th><td>daily</td><td>26 h 30 min</td><td>0 min</td><td>fresh</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Backup integrity check</th><td>weekly</td><td>8 days</td><td>0 min</td><td>fresh</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      </div>
      <ol class="edge-log" data-wd-log aria-live="polite"></ol>
    </div>
  </Panel>

  <SectionHead label="Recovery" title="Backups, timed" />
  <p>
    <strong>In one line:</strong> every guest is backed up twice, to different
    disks, and restores are drilled on a stopwatch. A backup that has never been
    restored is a hope.
  </p>
  <table class="ev-table">
    <caption>Backup layers, read from the live job configuration.</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">What</th><th scope="col">When</th><th scope="col">Where</th><th scope="col">How</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {ev.backups.map((b) => (
        <tr><th scope="row">{b.what}</th><td>{b.when}</td><td>{b.where}</td><td>{b.how}</td></tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="finds restores">
    {ev.restores.map((r) => (
      <article class="find" data-card>
        <p class="label">restore drill · {r.date}</p>
        <h3>{r.what}: {mmss(r.seconds)}</h3>
        <p>{r.check}</p>
        <p class="sample-note">Timed from the {r.source}.</p>
      </article>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p>
    Both drills restore into a throwaway copy with no network, so a restored
    service can never answer for the live one. Next drill: boot the restored
    applications on an isolated network, not only check their data.
  </p>

  <SectionHead label="Postmortems" title="Three things that broke, and what changed" />
  <p>
    Each looked fine until it didn't. For each: what was wrong, how I found it,
    the fix, and the proof the fix holds.
  </p>
  <div class="finds">
    <article class="find" data-card>
      <p class="label">memory · hypervisor · 10-26 to 10-29</p>
      <h3>The swap that kept coming back</h3>
      <p class="find-tldr">The usual fixes treated the symptom. Per-container counters found the one app causing 86 % of it.</p>
      <dl>
        <dt>Wrong</dt>
        <dd>Host swap hit 98.5 % with 16.7 GB of RAM free. Three textbook fixes (a smaller ZFS cache, more RAM for the container, swappiness 1) each held for about two hours.</dd>
        <dt>Found</dt>
        <dd>Per-cgroup counters instead of host totals: one container caused 86 % of all swap-outs. Inside it, one file-heavy app read its data through memory mapping, which parked 7.24 GiB of file pages on that container's memory bill, so the kernel pushed every other service out to swap.</dd>
        <dt>Fix</dt>
        <dd>Plain file I/O for that app, kernel watermark boosting off, a swap cap on the container, and a logger that records swap per guest every 10 minutes.</dd>
        <dt>Proof</dt>
        <dd>That app dropped from 7.3 GiB to 63 MB the moment the setting changed, and swap stayed flat overnight. The logger has since pointed at a second, smaller source, the SIEM VM, which is the next fix.</dd>
      </dl>
    </article>

    <article class="find" data-card>
      <p class="label">self-heal · 10-22</p>
      <h3>Self-heal rebooted the wrong VM</h3>
      <p class="find-tldr">Automation that guesses is dangerous. Now it checks who really owns an address before it acts.</p>
      <dl>
        <dt>Wrong</dt>
        <dd>A TV on the network grabbed the media VM's address. The probe saw the media server stop answering and rebooted the proxy VM, which was fine. During a planned reboot, the same script "repaired" 20 services that were only shutting down.</dd>
        <dt>Found</dt>
        <dd>The outage itself, then the repair log line by line.</dd>
        <dt>Fix</dt>
        <dd>Version 2 sends raw ARP requests to see who answers for each static address and raises an IP-conflict alert instead of acting. It reboots the proxy only when SSH to it fails, does nothing until the host reports itself up and has been up {sh.boot_guard_s / 60} minutes, and makes at most {sh.max_repairs} repairs in {sh.window_h} hours.</dd>
        <dt>Proof</dt>
        <dd>A container stopped on purpose on 10-30 was repaired in about 1 min 45 s, and Atlas announced it. {sh.probes} probes run every {sh.every_min} minutes, one pass in {sh.run_seconds} s.</dd>
      </dl>
    </article>

    <article class="find" data-card>
      <p class="label">disk-pressure cleanup · 10-30</p>
      <h3>A delete button on autopilot</h3>
      <p class="find-tldr">Caught a cleanup tool a month before it would have started deleting the wrong things.</p>
      <dl>
        <dt>Wrong</dt>
        <dd>The tool that frees space by deleting media was in automatic mode, blind to watch history since a media-server upgrade, and about a month from its first real run. Over 700 GB of library files were hardlinks to older copies, so a delete would free nothing and the next pass would delete more.</dd>
        <dt>Found</dt>
        <dd>An audit of its source, database and settings, before it had deleted anything.</dd>
        <dt>Fix</dt>
        <dd>Approval mode, so nothing goes without a click. A small internal shim that translates its auth header for the new media server. 2.1 TB of orphaned files moved aside with a restore manifest. An upstream bug report for a release that broke its own login page.</dd>
        <dt>Proof</dt>
        <dd>The delete path, proven end to end on a hardlinked test title: approval, 30-second queue, removal, import exclusion, audit entry. The media-server integration recovered after 9,266 failed calls.</dd>
      </dl>
    </article>
  </div>

  <SectionHead label="Decisions" title="Design decisions" />
  <p>Each of these was a choice with a reason, not a default I inherited.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Guests on a mirror, backups on other disks.</strong> One dead disk
      can never take a guest and its only backup together.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Restores are drills, with a stopwatch.</strong> {mmss(r1.seconds)} for
      the SIEM VM, {mmss(r2.seconds)} for the request database.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Default-deny nftables inside each guest</strong> instead of the
      cluster firewall. The guests stay portable, and the host is not the only
      layer.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Egress isolation that fails closed, and is tested.</strong> The
      workloads that must not leak share the VPN container's namespace, whose
      firewall drops anything not headed into the tunnel. Six tunnel kills, 45
      probes while it was down, zero answers.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>TLS terminates at one hardened proxy.</strong> Services are never
      exposed directly. <a href="/projects/security/">What guards it</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Automation repairs only what is safe to repeat.</strong> Self-heal
      and the watchdog alert on everything else, and anything destructive waits
      for a human.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Updates roll themselves back.</strong> Weekly image updates pass a
      health gate or return to the previous image; I proved that path by forcing
      a failed gate on a real service before trusting it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <script is:inline src="/js/homelab-watchdog.js" defer></script>
  <script is:inline src="/js/homelab-map.js" defer></script>
</ProjectLayout>
